.TH rcc 1 "august 2026" "rookcc 4.0.0" "user commands"
.SH name
rcc \- standalone native c compiler
.SH use
.B rcc
.RI [ options ] " file.c ..."
.SH description
rcc preprocesses parses checks optimizes and writes target machine code itself

for a target matching the host architecture and operating system rcc produces a
runnable executable in one command. x86 64 linux is linked internally. native
macos bsd and non x86 linux hosted executables use the platform compiler driver
only for the final sdk startup file and system library link
.SH options
.TP
.BI -o " file"
write output to file
.TP
.B -E
preprocess only
.TP
.B -S
write a machine code listing when the target supports it
.TP
.B -c
write a target elf or mach-o relocatable object
.TP
.B -run
compile and run a temporary native executable
.TP
.B --check
parse check and optimize without writing output
.TP
.B --emit-ir
write verified target independent ir
.TP
.BI -std= mode
select c90 c99 c11 c17 c23 gnu99 gnu11 gnu17 gnu23 or rcc
.TP
.BI --target= target
select an architecture and linux freebsd openbsd netbsd or macos target
.TP
.BI --sysroot= dir
use a target sysroot
.TP
.BR -O0 , " -O1" , " -O2" , " -O3" , " -Og" , " -Os" , " -Oz" , " -Ofast"
select optimization policy
.TP
.BR -I , " -iquote" , " -isystem" , " -D" , " -U"
control the preprocessor
.TP
.BR -L , " -l" , " -R" , " -Wl,-rpath,dir"
control native platform library and archive resolution
.TP
.B #pragma rcc link name
request a shared library from the source file
.TP
.B -static
write an executable without a program interpreter
.TP
.B -fPIC
with \fB-c\fR write an x86 64 or mach-o pic object
.TP
.B -g
write dwarf 4 function line and symbol data for x86 64 output
.TP
.B --print-targets
list targets
.TP
.B --print-backends
list backend scope
.TP
.B --print-toolchain
show compiler registries
.TP
.B --version
show version and target information
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
show complete help
.SH gnu assembly
gnu modes accept asm __asm and __asm__ with operands constraints clobbers volatile and asm goto

supported templates are encoded by the x86 64 backend
.SH target formats
freebsd openbsd and netbsd targets write elf64 relocatable objects and
freestanding static executables for x86 64 aarch64 and riscv64

openbsd executables include a PT_OPENBSD_SYSCALLS pin table

macos x86 64 and arm64 targets write native mach-o objects with \fB-c\fR

when target and host match rcc automatically invokes the native platform driver
and \fBrcc source.c -o program\fR produces a runnable hosted executable

cross macos hosted programs require \fB-c\fR followed by an apple sdk toolchain
link because rcc does not bundle an apple sdk and macos does not execute elf
.SH environment
.TP
.B RCC_PLATFORM_LINKER
override the compiler driver used only for a matching native hosted final link
.SH files
the default resource tree is /usr/local/share/rcc
